{
  "dom_id": "step-q52865",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945897/assets/f8f748278d73c52d292e1b0545798507768cced623291a88df9175a4188ee976.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $\u25b3ABC$ and $\u25b3CDE$ shown above. If the area of $\u25b3CDE$ is $18{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $\u25b3ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)",
  "worked_solution": "Let's consider $\u25b3CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{\u2015}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{\u2015}{DE}$ and that $DE=6\\text{\u00a0cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $\u25b3CDE$ is $18{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 18 & =\\frac{6\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 3CD & =18 \\\\ CD & =6\\text{\u00a0cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{\u2015}{CD}$ is also the height of $\u25b3ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{\u2015}{AB}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945897/assets/87c774cbdce6c4858605e2b8c6186abc6dde73b2b636966b4049ded4f82af684.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $\u25b3ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\  & =12{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
